Use @Image1 as the first frame.
This first frame sets the composition, camera position, scene and framing: a wider medium shot of the man at a white glass-panel door with a stainless deadbolt above a stainless knob, a frosted glass panel with blinds, a plant, and bright daylight. Keep this WIDE medium framing and camera for the entire shot; do NOT zoom in to a close-up.
A man in a plain denim shirt with a red carpenter's pencil over his ear crouches at the door and measures the door-lock backset. His hands and the yellow tape measure move through three beats in order, taking their position and pose from @Image2, then @Image3, then @Image4:
@Image2 is beat 1: the hook is caught on the edge of the door beside the latch and the tape is brought a short span across toward the knob.
@Image3 is beat 2: the hands hold the tape steady across the short distance from the door edge to the centre of the knob, measuring the backset.
@Image4 is beat 3: the hands keep the tape at the lockset with the short backset still shown.
Use @Image2, @Image3 and @Image4 only for the position and pose of the hands and the yellow tape over the lockset — a short span that never extends across the door or onto the glass panel — without changing the wide composition, camera or framing defined by @Image1.
The action flows continuously through beats 1, 2 and 3 with natural hand motion. Partway through, he clearly turns the door a few inches toward the camera so the lockset faces the viewer and the measurement is easier to see; the camera itself stays fixed and the wide medium framing is kept the whole time.
Maintain continuity in the white door, the stainless deadbolt and knob, the yellow-and-black tape measure, the man's denim shirt and red pencil, the plant, and the frosted glass panel.
